original_argument:
Si $g\in G_{\rho,\sigma}$, et
$\varepsilon=\varepsilon_G(g)$, $\varepsilon'=\varepsilon'_G(g)$, alors
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{int}(g)(\rho)&\underset{\mathcal G^+}{\sim}\rho^{\varepsilon'}
=\unsure{\tau^{\nu'}(\rho)},\\
\operatorname{int}(g)(\sigma)&\underset{\mathcal G^+}{\sim}\sigma^\varepsilon
=\unsure{\tau^\nu(\rho)|\tau^\nu(\sigma)}.
\end{aligned}
\]
\deleted{\unsure{donc}}
(où $(-1)^\nu=\varepsilon$, $(-1)^{\nu'}=\varepsilon'$), \inserted{donc}
\deleted{$\exists g\in\unsure{\mathcal G^+}$ tel que}
$\exists a=\tau^{\nu'}\mathbin{.}\unsure{a_0|ag}\in N_\rho$
(où $a_0=\unsure{\tau^\nu g}$) tel que

a) $g\equiv\unsure{a|a_0}\ (\mathcal G^+)$ et

b) $\varepsilon_\rho(a)=\unsure{\varepsilon'|\varepsilon'_G}
=\unsure{\varepsilon'_G(g)|\varepsilon_G(g)}$ ---

(et idem pour $N_\sigma$\unsure{\dots})

donc
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Im}\bigl(N_\rho^{\deleted{\unsure{*}}(+1)}
\xrightarrow{\deleted{\unsure{\delta_\rho}}}\gamma_{\rho,\sigma}\bigr)
&\supset\gamma'_{\rho,\sigma}\deleted{\unsure{\varepsilon'}},\\
\operatorname{Im}\bigl(N_\rho^{(-1)}\longrightarrow\gamma_{\rho,\sigma}\bigr)
&\supset\gamma_{\rho,\sigma}\setminus\gamma'_{\rho,\sigma}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Mais celà ne prouve \uline{pas} que l'on ait \unsure{commutativité}!
\unsure{d'où sans doute}

